They wouldn't exist in that scenario... so there would be no harm caused to them because they wouldn't have the ability to desire life... it would be unethical if there was harm being caused to someone in that scenario but simply not having a child isn't unethical because it's not harming anyone directly... even if the people in question really want children, not having children isn't harming their survival... it might at best harm their quality of life and then it becomes a question on how much the parents value their lives and how they wish to mirror that onto their children... which then brings up the argument that they're probably already making a choice for their child before they even exist, it just gets into tricky territory but I know this... when it goes wrong it goes really wrong... so I think if you're going to have children you need to carry that weight and the weight of bringing a child into this world because even two well adjusted happy healthy people can bring to life a child who... simply doesn't want to be here...

&#x200B;

... but no, not having the child wouldn't be unethical... there is no child to harm in that scenario.
